Part 1 — Generic Cancel (applies to every job, including future ones):
When spawning any job’s subprocess, launch it as its own process group (not just a single PID). Track the process group ID per run alongside existing run metadata. Add POST /api/jobs/{job_id}/runs/{run_id}/cancel: send SIGTERM to the entire process group, wait briefly, then SIGKILL if it hasn’t exited — this ensures child processes (kubectl calls, zip commands, mail sends, etc.) are also cleaned up, not left orphaned. Mark the run’s status as cancelled (a new distinct status, separate from success/failed) in both the in-memory run tracking and the on-disk history meta.json. Show a “Cancel” button on any job card while that job is actively running, and reflect the cancelled status with its own distinct badge color in both the live view and history list.
Part 2 — Reusable “custom job signal” mechanism (for graceful, job-specific stop behavior):
Add an optional signals field to a job’s entry in jobs.yaml, e.g. signals: [{name: stop_report, label: "Stop Report"}]. For each declared signal, expose POST /api/jobs/{job_id}/runs/{run_id}/signal/{signal_name}, which simply creates a flag file at logs/{job_id}/console_out/{run_number}/.signal_{signal_name}. The backend doesn’t need to know what a signal means — that’s entirely up to the job script, which is responsible for checking for that flag file at appropriate points in its own logic and reacting accordingly. Render a button for each declared signal on that job’s card (in addition to the generic Cancel button) while the job is running.
Part 3 — Apply this to the PgBadger job specifically:
Give it a stop_report signal (per Part 2). In incremental mode, on each 5-minute loop iteration, check for the .signal_stop_report flag file before starting the next cycle — if present, break out of the loop and continue on to the existing zip/transfer/email steps normally, as if the incremental collection period ended naturally. This is separate from Cancel: Cancel (Part 1) must abort the entire job immediately, skipping zip/transfer/email entirely, regardless of mode.
